from lib import *
D="d6"

L("6-01",D,"d6c1","Automation and Controller-Based Networking",["6.1","6.2","6.3"],40,
"Traditionally, engineers configure each device by hand through the CLI. Automation and controllers let you define what you want once and push it to many devices consistently. Software-Defined Networking (SDN) moves decision-making to a central controller.",
[(B,"Why automate","Manual configuration is slow, error-prone, and inconsistent. Automation reduces human error, speeds up changes, enforces standard configurations, and frees engineers for design work."),
 (I,"Planes and controllers","Data plane: forwarding packets/frames (switching, routing lookups, ACL filtering, NAT). Control plane: building the information used for forwarding (routing protocols, STP, ARP). Management plane: device management (SSH, SNMP, syslog). In traditional networking, each device runs its own control plane and is managed individually. In controller-based networking, a controller centralizes the control/management logic and programs devices. The controller exposes a northbound interface (NBI), usually REST APIs, to applications and scripts, and uses southbound interfaces (SBIs) such as NETCONF, RESTCONF, OpenFlow, OpFlex, SSH/CLI, or SNMP to talk to devices."),
 (X,"SDN architectures: underlay, overlay, fabric","Underlay: the physical network and its routing that provides IP reachability between devices. Overlay: virtual tunnels built on top of the underlay (e.g., VXLAN) that carry user traffic and create logical networks. Fabric: the combination of underlay and overlay managed as one system. In Cisco SD-Access, the underlay uses IS-IS, the overlay data plane uses VXLAN, the control plane uses LISP, and policy uses Cisco TrustSec (SGTs), all managed by Catalyst Center (formerly DNA Center). Traditional management: box-by-box, CLI, per-device changes. Controller-based (Catalyst Center): centralized intent-based policy, automation, assurance/analytics, and APIs.")],
"Modern enterprise, data center, and WAN networks are increasingly controller-driven. Understanding planes and APIs is required to work with them.",
[("Adding a VLAN to 200 switches","Traditional: log in to 200 switches. Controller-based: define the VLAN once in Catalyst Center or run one automation job that pushes it via APIs."),
 ("SD-WAN","A controller distributes routing and policy to branch routers; branches build encrypted overlay tunnels across any transport.")],
["Confusing northbound (to apps) and southbound (to devices) APIs.",
 "Thinking SDN removes the data plane from devices; devices still forward traffic.",
 "Mixing up underlay (physical) and overlay (virtual)."],
[("Data plane","Forwarding of user traffic."),
 ("Control plane","Protocols and processes that build forwarding tables."),
 ("Management plane","Protocols used to manage devices."),
 ("NBI / SBI","Northbound (controller-to-app) / Southbound (controller-to-device) interfaces."),
 ("Overlay / Underlay","Virtual tunnel network / physical transport network.")],
["Data plane forwards; control plane decides; management plane manages.",
 "NBI = REST APIs to applications; SBI = NETCONF, RESTCONF, OpenFlow, OpFlex, CLI, SNMP.",
 "SD-Access: IS-IS underlay, VXLAN data plane, LISP control plane.",
 "Catalyst Center = former DNA Center."],
["Expect to categorize functions into data/control/management planes."],
"Automation and controllers replace box-by-box management with centralized, API-driven control. Know the three planes, NBI vs SBI, and underlay/overlay/fabric concepts.",
[("Which plane does OSPF belong to?",["Data plane","Control plane","Management plane","Overlay plane"],1,"Routing protocols are control plane."),
 ("A Python script calls the controller's REST API. Which interface is used?",["Southbound","Northbound","Eastbound","Underlay"],1,"Applications use the northbound API."),
 ("In SD-Access, which protocol provides the overlay data plane?",["LISP","IS-IS","VXLAN","OSPF"],2,"VXLAN encapsulates overlay traffic; LISP is the control plane.")],
diagrams=["sdn"])

L("6-02",D,"d6c1","AI and Machine Learning in Network Operations",["6.4"],20,
"Artificial intelligence and machine learning help operations teams spot problems sooner and work faster. Predictive AI learns what 'normal' looks like and flags anomalies or forecasts issues; generative AI creates content such as summaries, explanations, or configuration drafts from natural-language requests.",
[(B,"Basic definitions","AI is the broad field of making machines perform tasks that normally need human intelligence. Machine learning (ML) is a subset of AI where systems learn patterns from data instead of being explicitly programmed. Supervised learning uses labeled data, unsupervised finds patterns in unlabeled data, and reinforcement learning learns through rewards."),
 (I,"Predictive AI in networking","Learns baselines from telemetry (latency, client onboarding times, interface errors) and detects anomalies, predicts capacity exhaustion or failures, and correlates events to suggest root causes. Example: network assurance platforms flag that DHCP onboarding time at one site deviates from its learned baseline."),
 (X,"Generative AI and responsible use","Generative AI (large language models) can answer natural-language questions about network state, summarize logs and incidents, draft configurations or documentation, and guide troubleshooting. Its output can be wrong (hallucinations), so engineers must validate generated configurations before deployment, protect sensitive data sent to AI services, and keep humans in control of changes.")],
"AI-assisted operations reduce mean time to detect and resolve problems in large networks, and the v1.1 blueprint added this topic explicitly.",
[("Predictive","An assurance tool predicts that a WAN link will exceed 90% utilization next month based on growth trends."),
 ("Generative","An engineer asks an AI assistant to summarize 2,000 syslog lines into the three most likely root causes, then verifies them manually.")],
["Treating generative AI output as automatically correct.",
 "Confusing predictive (forecast/anomaly) with generative (create content).",
 "Thinking ML requires manually written rules."],
[("Machine learning","Systems that learn patterns from data."),
 ("Predictive AI","Uses data to forecast outcomes and detect anomalies."),
 ("Generative AI","Creates new content (text, code, configs) from prompts."),
 ("Baseline","Learned 'normal' behavior used for anomaly detection.")],
["Predictive AI = baselines, anomaly detection, forecasting.",
 "Generative AI = creates content; must be validated by humans.",
 "ML types: supervised, unsupervised, reinforcement."],
["Objective 6.4 is conceptual: explain how AI (generative and predictive) and ML are used in network operations."],
"Predictive AI and ML learn baselines to detect anomalies and forecast issues; generative AI produces summaries, answers, and drafts that engineers must verify.",
[("Detecting that client onboarding time is abnormal compared to history is an example of:",["Generative AI","Predictive AI / ML","SNMP polling only","Static routing"],1,"Baselines and anomaly detection are predictive AI/ML."),
 ("Which risk is specific to generative AI output?",["Hallucinated/incorrect content","Too much encryption","Spanning tree loops","Duplex mismatch"],0,"Generated content may be plausible but wrong."),
 ("ML that learns from labeled examples is:",["Unsupervised","Supervised","Reinforcement","Generative"],1,"Supervised learning uses labeled data.")])

L("6-03",D,"d6c2","REST APIs and JSON",["6.5","6.7"],40,
"APIs let software talk to software. REST APIs use standard HTTP methods to create, read, update, and delete data, usually exchanging JSON. Controllers and modern network devices expose REST APIs so scripts can manage them.",
[(B,"REST and CRUD","REST APIs use URIs to identify resources and HTTP verbs as actions: POST = Create, GET = Read, PUT = Update/replace, PATCH = partial update, DELETE = Delete. Responses carry status codes: 200 OK, 201 Created, 204 No Content, 400 Bad Request, 401 Unauthorized (not authenticated), 403 Forbidden (not permitted), 404 Not Found, 500 Internal Server Error."),
 (I,"REST characteristics and authentication","Client-server, stateless (each request carries everything needed), cacheable, uniform interface, layered system (and optional code-on-demand). Authentication types: HTTP Basic (username/password, Base64-encoded, use only over HTTPS), API keys, bearer tokens (e.g., obtain a token then send it in an Authorization header), and OAuth. Data encoding: JSON, XML, or YAML, indicated by headers such as Content-Type: application/json."),
 (X,"JSON components","Objects are enclosed in { } and contain key:value pairs separated by commas. Keys are strings in double quotes. Values can be a string (\"Gi0/1\"), number (1500), boolean (true/false), null, an object, or an array. Arrays are ordered lists in [ ]. Example: {\"interface\": {\"name\": \"GigabitEthernet0/1\", \"enabled\": true, \"mtu\": 1500, \"ipv4\": [\"10.1.1.1/24\"]}}. JSON has no comments, trailing commas are invalid, and whitespace is insignificant.")],
"APIs and JSON are the language of automation. Even if you never write code, you must read API output and understand what a call does.",
[("Read device list","GET https://controller/api/v1/network-device with a token header → 200 OK and a JSON array of devices."),
 ("Count JSON elements","{\"vlans\": [10, 20, 30]} contains one object with one key whose value is an array of three numbers.")],
["Using single quotes in JSON (invalid; JSON requires double quotes).",
 "Mapping PUT to Create (POST creates; PUT replaces/updates).",
 "Confusing 401 (not authenticated) and 403 (authenticated but not allowed)."],
[("API","Application Programming Interface."),
 ("REST","Representational State Transfer architectural style using HTTP."),
 ("CRUD","Create, Read, Update, Delete."),
 ("JSON","JavaScript Object Notation, lightweight data format.")],
["CRUD ↔ HTTP: Create POST, Read GET, Update PUT/PATCH, Delete DELETE.",
 "Status codes: 2xx success, 4xx client error, 5xx server error.",
 "JSON: { } objects, [ ] arrays, \"key\": value, double quotes only."],
["You may be asked to identify the number of objects/arrays or the data type of a value in a JSON sample."],
"REST APIs perform CRUD via HTTP verbs, are stateless, and return status codes; JSON encodes data with objects, arrays, and typed values.",
[("Which HTTP verb maps to Read?",["POST","GET","PUT","DELETE"],1,"GET retrieves data."),
 ("What does a 401 response mean?",["Not found","Unauthorized — authentication required/failed","Server error","Created"],1,"401 = authentication required or failed."),
 ("In {\"up\": true}, what type is the value?",["String","Number","Boolean","Array"],2,"true is a JSON boolean.")],
diagrams=["rest_api"])

L("6-04",D,"d6c2","Configuration Management: Ansible and Terraform",["6.6"],25,
"Configuration management tools apply consistent configuration to many devices automatically. Ansible pushes changes over SSH or APIs using YAML playbooks without installing agents. Terraform describes infrastructure as code and builds it to match the declared state.",
[(B,"Infrastructure as Code","Instead of typing commands, you describe the desired configuration in text files stored in version control. The tool makes devices match the files, giving repeatable, reviewable, and reversible changes."),
 (I,"Ansible","Red Hat Ansible is agentless and push-based. A control node connects to managed devices over SSH (or NETCONF/REST APIs). Key files: inventory (list of devices/groups), playbooks (YAML files with plays and tasks), modules (units of work such as cisco.ios.ios_config), and variables/templates (Jinja2). Ansible is written in Python and is widely used for network configuration."),
 (X,"Terraform and comparison","HashiCorp Terraform is declarative infrastructure as code using HCL (HashiCorp Configuration Language). Providers interact with platforms (clouds, controllers, some network devices via APIs). Workflow: write → 'terraform plan' (preview changes) → 'terraform apply'. It tracks what it manages in a state file. Typical use: provisioning infrastructure (cloud networks, VMs, controller objects); Ansible typically configures devices and software. Older tools Puppet and Chef are agent-based and pull-based (supplementary; removed from v1.1).")],
"Configuration management is how large networks stay consistent and how changes are reviewed and rolled back.",
[("Ansible playbook","A playbook targets the 'access_switches' inventory group and ensures NTP servers and a login banner are configured on all of them."),
 ("Terraform","A Terraform file declares a cloud VPC, subnets, and security groups; 'terraform apply' creates them and records them in state.")],
["Thinking Ansible needs an agent on network devices.",
 "Confusing YAML (Ansible) with HCL (Terraform).",
 "Running 'terraform apply' without reviewing 'terraform plan'."],
[("Playbook","Ansible YAML file describing tasks to run on hosts."),
 ("Inventory","Ansible list of managed hosts and groups."),
 ("HCL","HashiCorp Configuration Language used by Terraform."),
 ("State file","Terraform's record of managed resources."),
 ("Agentless","No software installed on managed devices.")],
["Ansible: agentless, push, SSH/API, YAML playbooks, Python.",
 "Terraform: declarative IaC, HCL, providers, plan/apply, state file.",
 "Puppet/Chef (older): agent-based, pull model."],
["v1.1 objective 6.6: recognize capabilities of Ansible and Terraform."],
"Ansible pushes configuration agentlessly using YAML playbooks over SSH/APIs; Terraform declaratively provisions infrastructure using HCL, providers, and a state file.",
[("Which statement about Ansible is correct?",["Agent-based pull model","Agentless push model using SSH","Uses HCL","Requires SNMP"],1,"Ansible is agentless and pushes via SSH or APIs."),
 ("Which language does Terraform use?",["YAML","HCL","Ruby DSL","JSON only"],1,"HashiCorp Configuration Language."),
 ("Which Terraform command previews changes?",["terraform init","terraform plan","terraform apply","terraform push"],1,"plan shows what will change.")],
diagrams=[], supp=["Ansible can also use HTTP APIs and NETCONF modules, and Terraform can manage some network controllers; the exam focuses on the core characteristics listed above."])
